# canonscope -- keep the desktop's hands off the camera.
#
# THE PROBLEM
#   60-libgphoto2-6.rules matches any PTP still-image device (USB interface
#   class 060101) and sets ID_GPHOTO2=1. gvfs-gphoto2-volume-monitor watches
#   for exactly that and auto-mounts the camera, claiming the PTP session.
#   The camera screen goes black and chdkptp reports:
#       open_camera_dev_usb:usb_ptp_device_reset(): Device or resource busy
#       open_camera_dev_usb: ptp_opensession failed 0x2ff
#
# THE FIX
#   Clear the two markers for this one camera, so the volume monitor never
#   notices it. Other cameras keep working normally.
#
# WHY THE NUMBER IS 99 AND NOT, SAY, 61
#   It must sort after 60-libgphoto2-6.rules to override what that sets, and
#   after 70-uaccess.rules for a subtler reason: 70-uaccess.rules:17 reads
#       ENV{ID_GPHOTO2}=="?*", TAG+="uaccess"
#   so clearing ID_GPHOTO2 *before* that line runs would cost the desktop user
#   their ACL on the device node -- and with it, write access. Numbered at 99
#   the tag is already applied. (70-uaccess.rules:14 also grants uaccess from
#   the interface class alone, so this is belt and braces.)
#
# ACCESS IS GRANTED TWO WAYS, BECAUSE THE TWO MACHINES DIFFER
#   uaccess   an ACL for the user of the *active seat*. Works on a desktop.
#             Does NOT apply to an SSH session on a headless Pi: no seat,
#             no ACL.
#   plugdev   group ownership. The fallback that works headless -- the Pi's
#             user must be a member of plugdev, or it gets no write access
#             and chdkptp fails the same way gvfs made it fail.
#
# Setting MODE and GROUP here rather than relying on 60-libgphoto2-6.rules
# also means this works on a machine where libgphoto2 is not installed.

ACTION!="add|bind", GOTO="canonscope_end"

SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="04a9", ATTR{idProduct}=="31c0", \
    ENV{ID_GPHOTO2}="", ENV{GPHOTO2_DRIVER}="", \
    MODE="0664", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"

LABEL="canonscope_end"
